WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.845
Say you want to know what the city is like for
the typical member of an immigrant group.

2
00:00:05.494 --> 00:00:09.298
You collect one number for each city, like the
poverty rate.

3
00:00:09.348 --> 00:00:13.601
The easy move is to add them up and divide by
the number of cities.

4
00:00:14.251 --> 00:00:16.153
That is the plain average.

5
00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:18.406
It treats every city the same.

6
00:00:19.056 --> 00:00:22.078
The plain average of these three rates is 20%.

7
00:00:22.128 --> 00:00:26.285
But eight out of every ten people in this
group live in city C.

8
00:00:26.935 --> 00:00:31.276
A typical member does not live in a city at
20%.

9
00:00:31.326 --> 00:00:33.746
They live in a city at 30%.

10
00:00:34.396 --> 00:00:36.329
The fix is a weighted average.

11
00:00:36.379 --> 00:00:40.759
Give each city a weight equal to how many
group members live there.

12
00:00:41.409 --> 00:00:46.097
Multiply each city's rate by its weight.

13
00:00:46.147 --> 00:00:53.086
Add those up: 1,000, plus 2,000, plus 24,000,
makes 27,000.

14
00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:57.331
Add up the weights: 1,000.

15
00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:03.617
Divide: 27,000 over 1,000.

16
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:04.492
27%.

17
00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:09.801
That is the whole idea. The weighted average
describes the average member.

18
00:01:09.851 --> 00:01:12.664
The plain average describes the average city.

19
00:01:12.714 --> 00:01:16.292
When the group is spread unevenly, they can be
far apart.

20
00:01:16.942 --> 00:01:23.054
On your page, each number is built exactly
this way: two rows, and one division.

21
00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:29.281
Your page has fifty cities, not three, so its
numbers are bigger, but the rows are the same.

22
00:01:29.331 --> 00:01:33.745
Find them and check them, and you will know
where each number comes from.

23
00:01:34.396 --> 00:01:36.258
Now try the questions.
